Consider the Hamiltonian formulation of General Relativity in the extended phase space approach. In this approach, one starts from the Einstein–Hilbert action (possibly with matter), introduces gauge-fixing conditions through additional variables together with the corresponding ghost variables, and constructs an effective Lagrangian whose Hamiltonian formulation lives in an extended phase space that includes the gauge and ghost sectors alongside the original gravitational variables and their conjugate momenta. Within this framework one obtains:

(i) a Schrödinger-type equation for the wave function of the Universe that depends explicitly on the chosen reference frame (gauge);
(ii) a unitary evolution of the gravitating system in the extended phase space;
(iii) a consistent treatment of constraints without imposing them as operator restrictions on physical states (in contrast with Dirac quantization and the Wheeler–DeWitt equation);
(iv) the equivalence, at the classical level, with the standard Hamiltonian formulation of General Relativity after the gauge-fixing conditions are imposed.

These results have been established for cosmological and other minisuperspace-type models, i.e. for systems possessing only finitely many degrees of freedom obtained from General Relativity by symmetry reduction.

Problem. Extend the construction and the four properties (i)–(iv) above to full (3+1)-dimensional General Relativity, regarded as a field theory with infinitely many degrees of freedom. Concretely, build an extended phase space quantization of the full gravitational field, including a precise definition of the path integral measure, the associated ghost sector, and the gauge-dependent Hamiltonian; and prove that the resulting theory satisfies the analogues of (i)–(iv), in particular yielding a gauge-dependent Schrödinger equation for the wave functional of the gravitational field together with unitary evolution in the extended phase space.

